Carafe bench: the accelerated DMA copies between a buffer and an image count a remapped element in bytes. With
remapping the copy engine moves elements of components x component size bytes, and NVK uploads BC textures that
way: 16-byte elements, a line length of 64 for a 256x256 BC3 level. The software path multiplies by the element
size, but the accelerated path passed one byte per pixel, so the texture cache divided the line length and the
origin by the 16-byte block: 64 / 16 = 4 blocks per row reached an image that DXVK had cleared before, and the
game's textures sampled almost entirely black. Widths, origins and lengths now reach the accelerated path in
bytes; an origin that no longer fits its 16 bits takes the software path.
GPL-3.0-or-later, as the files it changes.

